I now have a complete, thorough understanding of all the patterns in the file. Here is a detailed breakdown.

---

## Pattern Analysis: `state-success` and `highlightHotspot` in `warp-engine/scenes.json`

The file is 374 lines and contains the full picture. There are **two distinct levels** of this pattern, and importantly this game does **not** use `highlightHotspot` on the individual scene arrows after puzzles are solved -- that only appears in two specific places. Here is the precise breakdown.

---

### Pattern 1 — goTo arrows with `state-success` after puzzle solved (main-room hub)

The "main-room" hub scene holds all the portal/arrow hotspots that lead to each scientist scene. Each `goTo` hotspot has a `states` array: when the corresponding `solved_*` flag is set, the hotspot gains the CSS class `state-success` and renders a `✓` checkmark overlay. This is the green-check pattern.

**Full `main-room` scene (lines 49-98):**

```json
{
  "id": "main-room",
  "title": "Řeka času",
  "image": "assets/scenes/time-river.jpg",
  "hotspots": [
    {
      "type": "goTo", "target": "scn-newton", "rect": {"x":82.55,"y":49.49,"w":17.45,"h":46},
      "states": [
        { "requireFlags": ["solved_newton"], "cssClass": "state-success", "content": "✓" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "goTo", "target": "scn-volta", "rect": {"x":71.34,"y":53,"w":10.73,"h":20.53},
      "states": [
        { "requireFlags": ["solved_volta"], "cssClass": "state-success", "content": "✓" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "goTo", "target": "scn-faraday", "rect": {"x":56.67,"y":59.86,"w":11.13,"h":23.18},
      "states": [
        { "requireFlags": ["solved_faraday"], "cssClass": "state-success", "content": "✓" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "goTo", "target": "scn-hertz", "rect": {"x":64.39,"y":34.18,"w":8.52,"h":19.96},
      "states": [
        { "requireFlags": ["solved_hertz"], "cssClass": "state-success", "content": "✓" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "goTo", "target": "scn-currents", "rect": {"x":44.78,"y":33.61,"w":14.99,"h":24.11},
      "states": [
        { "requireFlags": ["solved_currents"], "cssClass": "state-success", "content": "✓" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "goTo", "target": "scn-einstein", "rect": {"x":77.53,"y":18.09,"w":11.13,"h":21.46},
      "states": [
        { "requireFlags": ["solved_einstein"], "cssClass": "state-success", "content": "✓" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "goTo",
      "target": "warp-core",
      "rect": {"x":47.59,"y":2.35,"w":23.51,"h":24.39},
      "requireItems": [ "grav-key","volta-pile","induction-coil","radio-antenna","ac-transformer","relativistic-clock" ],
      "showNeedHint": true
    }
  ]
}
```

Key observations:
- The `goTo` arrow hotspot itself receives `state-success`. It is both the navigation arrow AND the puzzle-completion indicator.
- There is NO `highlightHotspot` event fired on these arrows after each scientist puzzle is solved. The `state-success` visual is purely declarative/flag-driven.
- The final warp-core `goTo` uses `requireItems` instead of `requireFlags` for its gate condition.

---

### Pattern 2 — `apply` item-slot hotspots with `state-success` + `clickable: false` (warp-core)

The `warp-core` scene has six item-deposit slots. Each uses `type: "apply"`, and after the item is consumed, `state-success` + `clickable: false` locks the slot.

**Representative example (two slots, lines 238-263):**

```json
{
  "type": "apply",
  "rect": { "x": 8, "y": 62, "w": 12, "h": 22 },
  "acceptItems": [ { "id": "grav-key", "consume": true } ],
  "onApply": { "setFlags": ["slot_gravity_ok"], "message": "Gravitační uzávěr zajištěn." },
  "states": [
    { "requireFlags": ["slot_gravity_ok"], "cssClass": "state-success", "content": "✓", "clickable": false }
  ]
},
{
  "type": "apply",
  "rect": { "x": 22, "y": 62, "w": 12, "h": 22 },
  "acceptItems": [ { "id": "volta-pile", "consume": true } ],
  "onApply": { "setFlags": ["slot_power_ok"], "message": "Primární napájení online." },
  "states": [
    { "requireFlags": ["slot_power_ok"], "cssClass": "state-success", "content": "✓", "clickable": false }
  ]
}
```

The difference from Pattern 1: `"clickable": false` is added so the slot becomes inert once used.

---

### Pattern 3 — `highlightHotspot` on intro scene (lines 291-303)

The only use of `highlightHotspot` on a puzzle/action spot is in the very first scene. It fires once when entering "intro" and pulses the "start" apply-hotspot for 8 seconds to tell the player what to click first.

```json
{
  "id": "dlg-intro-start",
  "once": true,
  "when": { "on": "enterScene", "scene": "intro" },
  "then": {
    "openDialog": { "id": "intro.start" },
    "highlightHotspot": {
      "sceneId": "intro",
      "rect": {"x":74.64,"y":81.82,"w":19.33,"h":16.74},
      "ms": 8000
    }
  }
}
```

---

### Pattern 4 — `highlightHotspot` on a goTo arrow after a condition is met (lines 333-344)

This is the only example where `highlightHotspot` fires on a navigation/goTo-style hotspot after a game state change. It fires when `warp_ready_dialog_done` is set, and it highlights the warp-jump `goTo` hotspot in `warp-core` for 15 seconds.

```json
{
  "id": "dlg-warp-ready-outro",
  "once": true,
  "when": { "on": "stateChange", "requireFlags": ["warp_ready_dialog_done"] },
  "then": {
    "highlightHotspot": {
      "sceneId": "warp-core",
      "rect": {"x":29.75,"y":35.4,"w":42.03,"h":16.38},
      "ms": 15000
    }
  }
}
```

The `rect` `{"x":29.75,"y":35.4,"w":42.03,"h":16.38}` matches exactly the `goTo` hotspot in `warp-core` that leads to the `exit` scene (line 232-236). So once all slots are filled, the engine sets `warp_engine_active`, the player finishes the victory dialog (which sets `warp_ready_dialog_done`), and then the `exit` goTo arrow glows for 15 seconds.

---

### Summary of which hotspot gets which treatment

| Scene | Hotspot type | Gets `state-success` | Gets `highlightHotspot` |
|---|---|---|---|
| `main-room` goTo arrows (x6) | `goTo` | Yes, when `solved_*` flag set | No |
| `warp-core` item slots (x6) | `apply` | Yes, when `slot_*_ok` flag set, + `clickable:false` | No |
| `intro` start hotspot | `apply` | No | Yes, on `enterScene`, 8000ms |
| `warp-core` exit arrow | `goTo` | No | Yes, on `stateChange` of `warp_ready_dialog_done`, 15000ms |

The two mechanisms serve different UX roles: `state-success` is a permanent, declarative, flag-driven overlay that persists (the green check stays). `highlightHotspot` is a temporary timed pulse used for onboarding guidance (intro) or the final "you can go now" signal (warp exit arrow).

